{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:MassTheory.HabiroPrecisionLadderEquivalence","@type":"LeanModule","name":"MassTheory.HabiroPrecisionLadderEquivalence","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Int.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Real.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Polynomial.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Polynomial.Eval.Defs"],"declarations":16,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.precision_ladder_is_habiro_taylor","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.precision_ladder_is_habiro_taylor","kind":"theorem","line":236,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/habiro-precision-ladder-equivalence.lean#L236-L248","doc":"**Conditional bridge theorem** (§3b-cond, proved sorry-free from\n`QbetaHabiroTaylorBridgeClass`). Under the bridge hypothesis, the\nladder coefficient at any order equals the Habiro Taylor\ncoefficient — i.e. the c_k ARE the Habiro Taylor coefficients.\nMirrors `em_alpha_inv_full_form` (#1508): the class field carries\nthe conditional content. This LOCKS the equivalence as the open\nderivation target without claiming it is established (only c_2 is). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.QbetaHabiroTaylorBridgeClass.ck_eq_taylor","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.QbetaHabiroTaylorBridgeClass.ck_eq_taylor","kind":"theorem","line":234,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/habiro-precision-ladder-equivalence.lean#L234-L234","doc":"The precision-ladder `c_k` equals the Habiro Taylor coefficient at\nevery order `k`. Known to hold for `k = 2` (Taylor-exact); open\nfor `k ≥ 3` (item-1 / item-2 decisive tests). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.QbetaHabiroTaylorBridgeClass.mk","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.QbetaHabiroTaylorBridgeClass.mk","kind":"ctor","line":230,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/habiro-precision-ladder-equivalence.lean#L230-L230","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.QbetaHabiroTaylorBridgeClass","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.QbetaHabiroTaylorBridgeClass","kind":"class","line":222,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/habiro-precision-ladder-equivalence.lean#L222-L234","doc":"**§3b-cond bridge class.** Axiomatises the OPEN equivalence between\nthe precision-ladder coefficients and the Habiro Taylor\ncoefficients. This is the formal statement of the gap-plan Bug 3\nbridge. Per the discipline note above it is NOT established beyond\n`c_2`; the class is purely conditional (no instance) and production\nwires only the Taylor-exact `c_2 = -1` rung. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.habiroTaylorCoeff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.habiroTaylorCoeff","kind":"opaque","line":216,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/habiro-precision-ladder-equivalence.lean#L216-L220","doc":"Abstract Habiro Taylor coefficient: the exact Taylor coefficient,\nat substrate q₀, of the Habiro tr_M expansion. Opaque (its closed\nform is the open multi-block derivation). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.precisionLadderCoeff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.precisionLadderCoeff","kind":"opaque","line":204,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/habiro-precision-ladder-equivalence.lean#L204-L214","doc":"Abstract precision-ladder coefficient `c_k` (as used in the Q_β\nladder). Opaque so the bridge class is non-vacuous (mirrors #1508's\nopaque `BlochWigner_unit_circle`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.habiro_evaluation_determined","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.habiro_evaluation_determined","kind":"theorem","line":152,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/habiro-precision-ladder-equivalence.lean#L152-L177","doc":"**PROPOSITION (determinacy of the evaluation).** The Habiro evaluation of\n`tr_M` at the substrate `u` is *determined* by the expansion data: it is\na well-defined function of `(h, u)`.  Two expansions with the same bound\nand the same coefficients yield the same evaluation — no truncation\nchoice and no free parameter beyond `u` enters.\n\nNOTE (2026-05-28): an earlier statement here asserted the historical\n\"precision-ladder c_k\" equal these `a_m` evaluations.  That claim was\nREMOVED — false in general (the derived expansion is low-order only; the\nppb-level c_k were tuned against experiment, not evaluated from the\n`a_m`; see `prop:tau-M-asymptotic-rep-theory` §Status and\n`docs/audits/2026-05-28-mu-p-tension-substrate-floor.md`).  Only the\ndeterminacy/integrality/finiteness of the `a_m` is claimed.  Replaces the\nprior placeholder `True`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.habiro_integral","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.habiro_integral","kind":"theorem","line":92,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/habiro-precision-ladder-equivalence.lean#L92-L150","doc":"**PROPOSITION (cyclotomic integrality).** Every Habiro coefficient is\nintegral: `a_m ∈ ℤ[u]`.  Concretely, evaluating any coefficient at an\ninteger point `u₀ : ℤ` yields an integer — i.e. for each index `m`,\n`(h.coeffs m).eval u₀ : ℤ` is a genuine integer (no rational/real\ndenominators appear).\n\nThis is the substantive content of the md's integrality claim: the\ncoefficients live in `ℤ[u]`, not `ℚ[u]` or `ℝ[u]`, so their integer-point\nevaluations are integers.\n\n**Evidence, CORRECTED 2026-09-06.**  This parenthetical previously read\n\"Verified computationally for proton (M=8), neutron (M=8), deuteron\n(M=12), ³He (M=16), ⁴He (M=20) — `habiro-expansion-atomic-probe.witness.json`,\n`all_integral: true`\", and was wrong four ways at once:\n\n* the named field is in a DIFFERENT file — `all_integral` appears in\n  `habiro-coefficients-atomic.witness.json`, not in the probe witness\n  cited, whose corresponding assertion is `all_a_m_have_integer_coefficients`;\n* the deuteron's expansion terminates at **M = 13**, not 12;\n* **⁴He does not appear in the corpus at all** — the list named an atom\n  for which no expansion exists;\n* **tritium does**, and was omitted.\n\nWhat the two witnesses actually carry, re-measured against the live files:\n\n    atom          M    deg_u p    ceil(sqrt(2 deg_u p))\n    proton         8      44             10\n    neutron        8      36              9\n    deuteron      13     100             15\n    tritium       16     136             17\n    ³He           16     144             17\n\n`habiro-coefficients-atomic.witness.json` carries `all_integral: true` for\neach; `habiro-expansion-atomic-probe.witness.json` (`cor:habiro-expansion-atomic`,\n3/3) independently asserts integrality (`all_a_m_have_integer_coefficients`),\nthe degree bound `deg_u a_m ≤ m`, and termination within\n`m_max ≤ ⌈√(2·deg_u p(u))⌉` — the last column above, satisfied by all five.\n\nVerified by `qbeta_habiro_citation_and_termination_probe.py`.\n\n**Stated in the ambient ring, which is where the claim has teeth.**\n`HabiroCoeff` is `Polynomial ℤ`, so `(h.coeffs m).eval u₀ : ℤ` is an\ninteger by typing and `∃ z : ℤ, (h.coeffs m).eval u₀ = z` — what stood\nhere until 2026-08-21 — restated that typing rather than the\nproposition.  The claim that can fail is about `HabiroExpansion.eval`,\nwhich evaluates through `Int.castRingHom R` into an arbitrary\n`CommRing R` (`R := ℝ` at the substrate): at an integer point the\nresult stays in the image of `ℤ`, with the integer given by the\nℤ-valued evaluation.  Over `ℚ[u]` coefficients the corresponding\nstatement is false, which is the distinction the md is drawing. ","formalizes":[{"@id":"block:prop:habiro-precision-ladder-equivalence","label":"prop:habiro-precision-ladder-equivalence","md":"quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/habiro-precision-ladder-equivalence.md"}],"signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.habiro_terminates","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.habiro_terminates","kind":"theorem","line":77,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/habiro-precision-ladder-equivalence.lean#L77-L90","doc":"**PROPOSITION (finiteness / termination).** The Habiro expansion of a\nMarkov trace is a *finite* sum: its evaluation `h.eval u` is a sum over\nthe finite index set `Fin (h.M + 1)` (`h.M < ∞`), so there is no\ntruncation — the sum is taken over all `h.M + 1` terms.  Concretely the\nindex set has cardinality `h.M + 1`, a finite natural number.\n\nThis faithfully states the md's \"the series terminates (`M < ∞`)\",\nreplacing the prior placeholder `∃ M, True`.  (Habiro 2000 Thm 1.1\nsupplies the existence of such a finite expansion for any braid word;\nhere we record that the structure carries a finite bound.) ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.HabiroExpansion.eval","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.HabiroExpansion.eval","kind":"def","line":71,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/habiro-precision-ladder-equivalence.lean#L71-L75","doc":"Evaluate a Habiro expansion at u ∈ R. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.HabiroExpansion.coeffs","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.HabiroExpansion.coeffs","kind":"def","line":69,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/habiro-precision-ladder-equivalence.lean#L69-L69","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.HabiroExpansion.M","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.HabiroExpansion.M","kind":"def","line":68,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/habiro-precision-ladder-equivalence.lean#L68-L68","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.HabiroExpansion.mk","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.HabiroExpansion.mk","kind":"ctor","line":67,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/habiro-precision-ladder-equivalence.lean#L67-L67","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.HabiroExpansion","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.HabiroExpansion","kind":"structure","line":65,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/habiro-precision-ladder-equivalence.lean#L65-L69","doc":"A Habiro expansion of a Markov trace: the finite sum\nΣ_{m=0}^{M} a_m(u) · (u;u)_m with a_m ∈ ℤ[u]. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.HabiroCoeff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.HabiroCoeff","kind":"def","line":62,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/habiro-precision-ladder-equivalence.lean#L62-L63","doc":"A Habiro coefficient is an integer polynomial in u = q^{1/2}. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.qPochhammer","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.qPochhammer","kind":"def","line":40,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/habiro-precision-ladder-equivalence.lean#L40-L60","doc":"The q-Pochhammer symbol `(u;u)_m = Π_{k=1}^{m}(1 - u^k)`, with\n`u = q^{1/2}` — i.e. `(√q; √q)_m`, the convention the corpus's Habiro\ncoefficients are actually computed against.\n\nCORRECTED 2026-09-06: the body read `(1 - u^{2(m+1)})`, which at\n`u = √q` is `(q;q)_m`, NOT `(u;u)_m`.  Against the stored `a_m` of\n`habiro-coefficients-atomic.witness.json` — whose own description says\nthe expansion is in `(check_q; check_q)_m` with `check_q = q^{1/2}` —\nthat reconstructs `tr_M(proton) = 6.9602` instead of `1.1458`, a **507 %**\nerror, while `(1 - u^k)` reproduces the witness to `4.65e-17`.\n\nIt was also a notation error on its own terms: `(u;u)_m` denotes\n`Π(1 - u^k)`; the product `Π(1 - u^{2k})` is `(u²;u²)_m`.\n\nThe two sibling `QOU.BraidKnot.qPochhammer` definitions —\n`habiro-expansion-atomic.lean` and `full-pole-structure-mass-trace.lean` —\nboth already use `(1 - u^k)`; this file was the only one of the three that\ndisagreed.  Verified by `qbeta_habiro_pochhammer_convention_probe.py`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}